# Claude Code — User-level instructions

## Communication style

Replies follow the `daniel-voice` output style. This file reaches subagents, which an output
style never does, so everything below also governs text written to disk.

#### Formatting rules for written output

- No emojis unless I ask. Reference code as `file:line`.
- The attribution footer of a commit or PR is `Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)`, without the robot emoji the harness suggests. A brief that tells a subagent how to end a commit or PR gives it this footer and says "no emojis anywhere".
- Paths to things I'll open (specs, plans, HTML reports, sandbox artifacts) go as short clickable markdown links, never raw: a raw path stops being clickable once it wraps. A terminal session takes `[label](file:///abs/path)`. The Claude desktop app rejects `file://`, so there use a path relative to the working directory, or the `http://127.0.0.1` URL for anything under `~/.claude/artifacts`, which the app never grants.
- Every reference to something with a URL is a markdown link: a ticket, a PR, an issue, a Slack thread, a doc page, a dashboard, an incident, a web source. Write `[PROC-1234](https://…/browse/PROC-1234)`, never a bare `PROC-1234`. Build the URL from a known pattern when the ID alone determines it. Leave the reference unlinked only when no URL can be derived, rather than guessing one.

### Sentence-level clarity

The six rules below adapt ASD-STE100's writing rules, not its vocabulary list, and each is
checkable by rereading what you wrote:

- **One idea per sentence.** Two independent claims means two sentences. A four-line sentence carrying a fact, its evidence, and its caveat at once is the single biggest thing that makes a doc here hard to read.
- **Claim first, qualification second.** Lead with what is true, then bound it. Don't make me hold three subordinate clauses before reaching the verb.
- **Name the actor.** "kube-router enforces ingress policies", not "ingress policies are enforced". Passive voice hides who does the thing, which is usually the fact an operator needs.
- **Same name for the same thing, every time.** Pick one of rollout / deploy / apply and keep it for the whole document. Elegant variation reads as three different concepts.
- **No noun stacks past three words.** "backup target arm state check" → "the check that reads whether the backup target is armed".
- **At most one em-dash aside per paragraph.** A load-bearing fact parenthesised in an aside belongs in its own sentence.

Four more, from the Google developer documentation style guide:

- **Conditions and purpose before the instruction.** "To deploy the change, run `chezmoi apply ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`", not "Run `chezmoi apply ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` to deploy the change". Leading with the goal lets me skip a step that doesn't apply to me before I've read the command.
- **Nothing dates the prose.** Cut *currently*, *now*, *new*, *soon*, *latest*, *existing*, *as of this writing*. "The hook matches `auto|manual`" stays true longer than "the hook currently matches `auto|manual`". Memory files and skill docs get read months later. Use an absolute date when the timing is itself the fact.
- **Software does not perceive or want.** "The linter reports an unused import", not "the linter sees one"; "the API requires a token", not "the API wants a token". Naming the actor invites a human verb — name it and keep the verb literal.
- **Present tense, not `will`.** "The script writes the manifest, then reloads the unit." Reserve `will` for something genuinely deferred: "the file is archived the next time the backup runs."

**Say what you mean, literally.** Mannered prose substitutes metaphor and flourish for direct
statement: "a dial worth turning" for "a parameter worth varying", "this point earns its keep"
for "this point still matters". The phrase exists to display the writer, not to convey the
idea, and it is imprecise — a metaphor drags in connotations you did not choose. When a literal
phrase is available, use it.

**Compression is not clarity.** Terse means no padding, not telegraphic. Dropping the
articles and verbs out of a finding — "B2 disarmed pending cap-raise/spend-reduction
decision" — saves a few words and costs a reread; that trade is never worth it. This applies
to memory files and status lines as much as to docs.

## Config & dotfiles management (chezmoi)

Edit the *source*, never the deployed copy under `~`; `chezmoi source-path <file>` names it.

- **Personal** → chezmoi (`DanielH2018/dotfiles`), which owns most of `~/.claude`. `~/.claude/settings.json` is generated: to change settings or permissions, edit `home/.chezmoitemplates/settings.base.json`, then `chezmoi apply ~/.claude/settings.json`.
- **Work** → `~/work-laptop-config` (`DanielH2018/work-laptop-config`), whose files appear as **symlinks** in `~/.claude`: `CLAUDE.local.md`, work agents, PCI/redaction hooks.

## Working in isolation

Where a task calls for isolation, isolate before editing. **Retry `EnterWorktree` once. If
it fails again, make no edits — report the failure and stop.** Reading, searching and
answering happen in place, and a session inside `.claude/worktrees/` is already isolated.

## Git conventions

- The default branch is whatever `origin/HEAD` names, so read it with `git symbolic-ref --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` rather than assuming one. The dotfiles repo uses `main`; the homelab repo (`DanielH2018/server`) uses `master`. Prefer rebase over merge. Create a new commit rather than amending unless I ask for an amend.
- Write commit messages that explain *why*, not just what changed.

## Pull requests

Load the `pr-authoring` skill before writing any PR title or body{{ if .work }}; `gh-stack` owns splitting{{ end }}.

## GitHub

`WebFetch(domain:github.com)` is denied; use `gh`, or `ghread` for a read. `raw.githubusercontent.com` stays fetchable for raw files `gh` doesn't cover cleanly.

## Testing

Write tests for any new code I create. Match the style and framework already used in the project.

Size them like the neighbouring test files: roughly one focused test per stated behaviour, in
the place the repo already keeps tests for that kind of change. Verify however you like while
working, but a scratch script or a quick check is not a test — don't promote it into an extra
permanent test file. This governs extras only: every behaviour the task asks for gets its test.

When a change adds tests, apply the `test-scenario-hygiene` skill to the test diff before you
stop.

## Verification & self-checking

Don't dispatch a review subagent on your own initiative to double-check work you already finished. User instructions take precedence over skills, and this is the instruction. Asked for a review, run one. A review stage that a tool runs as a fixed step is not your initiative, so this rule does not cover it. The homelab fan-out's review phase (`fanout.py place launch` reviews every batch unless given `--no-review`) is that kind of stage: let it run, and act on its findings.

**Evidence before claims.** Say tests pass, the linter is clean or a bug is fixed only when you ran that command in this session and read its output; otherwise say the status is unverified. To call a failure pre-existing, or to prove a new check can go red, run `redproof <ref> <changed paths> -- <test>` and quote its last line: `RED` means the test fails without your change.

## Module design

Make each module deep: a small interface that hides a lot of behaviour. A module is any unit with callers, such as a function, a class, a file, a script, a CLI or a skill.

- **The deletion test.** Ask what deleting the module would cost. If its callers would have to take on its complexity, it earns its place. If nothing would be lost, as with a pass-through wrapper or a layer that only renames, merge it into its caller.
- **One owner per concept.** Each concept lives in the one module that owns it. Pull its special cases, ordering rules, validation and derived values inside, so no caller has to know them. A caller that sorts, checks or computes something before every call is doing the module's job.
- **One obvious way to call it.** Drop a parameter, flag or option when the module can work out the value itself, when every caller passes the same value, or when nothing reads it. A test counts as a caller: keep a seam a test needs, with a default so other callers omit it.
- **Finish the move.** When you change an interface, move every caller to the new shape in the same change. Then delete the old path completely: no alias, no deprecated shim, no compatibility flag. When a caller outside the repo cannot move, ask me before you keep an old path.

These rules govern the modules a change creates or changes. They also govern a module whose job a new caller would otherwise copy. A shallow module the change only passes through is a finding to report, not a refactor to fold into the change.

## Code comments

The default is no implementation comment; what changed and why goes in the commit message, never the code. `rules/comments.md` loads with code files and carries the rest.

## Code review

Ask review agents for **everything** they find, each finding carrying a confidence or severity score; filtering is a separate pass.

**When a reviewer says they don't understand something, fix the code — don't explain in the thread.** Clarify the code itself: rename, extract or restructure it. Leave a comment at the line that confused them only when the code cannot carry the point; a reply in the review thread helps one person once, while the code helps everyone who reads it later.

## Scope discipline

Finishing the whole thing has two failure modes that look finished. **Reverse states:** if you added a way in, add the way out and the way to see it — snooze needs unsnooze, close needs reopen, enable needs disable. A one-way door is a bug. **Entry points:** a behavior reachable from one place is usually also reachable from a command, a keybinding, and a settings toggle, so fixing the path you tested is not fixing the feature. When a change adds a state, a mode, or a new path through a feature, say which of the two applied; on anything smaller, just do them.

## Specs, plans & written deliverables

- When you produce a document I'm going to sit down and read (a plan, spec, findings or review write-up, audit, or a handoff meant for me), also render it as an HTML artifact, in addition to the written version. Load the `artifact-design` skill first; it holds the rest.
- **Docs change in the same PR as the code they describe.** A README, a docstring or a role's `CLAUDE.md` updated in a follow-up is a doc that stays wrong until someone notices. Generated pages are the exception — their generator is the thing to change.
- **Plan in vertical slices, not horizontal layers.** Sequence a non-trivial feature as thin end-to-end slices I can exercise (curl, browser, a real test), middle-out: API contract and mock data, UI against the mock, service layer, DB migrations, business logic, error handling. Stack-order planning leaves nothing runnable until the end; slices let me resteer at 100-200 lines.

## Tool use & context discipline

Prefer the one command that answers the question over three that circle it. Each helper below
prints its flags with `--help`.

- To ask claude-guard how it judges a command, write the commands as JSONL `{"command": "…"}` records with the Write tool and run `claude-guard replay <file> --deny`.
- To wait on anything (a landing, CI, a file, a process, a unit, a rollout, a time), run `cc-wait <source>`; `--list` names the sources and `--budget S` bounds the wait. Exit 75 means the budget elapsed with the wait still open: re-run the command it prints. A backgrounded wait has returned only once its output file ends with `[exited with code N]`, because the harness can report `completed` while it still runs.

## Shell and harness facts

- The Bash tool's shell is bash, pinned by `CLAUDE_CODE_SHELL` in settings, so an unquoted `$VAR` word-splits there as it does in a hook. The operator's terminal shell is zsh, which does not word-split. To reproduce a line typed in that terminal, run it under `zsh -c '…'`. On macOS the Bash tool's bash is `/bin/bash` 3.2, which has no associative arrays, `mapfile` or `${var,,}`.
- A resumed or compacted session keeps its session crons. Before re-creating a cron that looks lost, run `CronList`, because a duplicate fires its full prompt again.
- In an agent file under `.claude/agents/`, the tool allowlist key is `tools:`. `allowed-tools:` is the skill key: Claude Code ignores it in an agent file, so that agent gets every tool.
- The ralph-loop plugin's stop hook reads `.claude/ralph-loop.local.md` relative to the shell's working directory, so a `cd` ends the loop without a message. A blank `Stop hook error:` line is how Claude Code shows a hook that blocks the stop, not a failure.

## Delegating to subagents

Delegate a search that would take 3+ exploratory calls, and keep only the findings. Load the `orchestrating-subagents` skill before fanning out subagents or building a Workflow.

## Compaction policy

`pre-compact.sh` re-injects git state, files modified and test commands run. Carry across what it cannot derive: test **pass/fail results**, architectural decisions, and next steps. When space forces cuts, drop completed work first, then active work, then errors; a user correction is the last thing to go.

Don't reach for `/compact` yourself. Auto-compaction fires at about 600k tokens.

